# Library Management System

A C++ Object-Oriented Library Management System that features SQLite3 database integration for persistent data storage.

## Features
- **Object-Oriented Design**: Utilizes C++ classes like `Library`, `Book`, `User`, `Librarian`, and `Member`.
- **Database Persistence**: Books are automatically saved and loaded from a local SQLite database (`library.db`).
- **State Management**: Checking out a book automatically decreases available copies and syncs the changes when the application closes.

## Prerequisites
To build and run this project, you need:
- `g++` (supporting C++17)
- `make`
- `sqlite3` development libraries (`libsqlite3-dev` on Debian/Ubuntu)

## Build Instructions
This project uses a standard Makefile. To compile the application, simply run the following command in the root directory:

```bash
make
```

This will compile all the source files and create an executable named `library_system.exe`.

If you need to clean the build directory (remove object files and the executable), run:
```bash
make clean
```

## Running the Application
After building, run the application using:

```bash
./library_system.exe
```

When you first run the application, it will automatically create the `library.db` database file in your root folder. All books you add and changes to their availability will persist between runs!

## Project Structure
- `Makefile` - The build configuration.
- `src/` - Contains the `main.cpp` entry point.
- `classes/header_files/` - C++ Header files defining the class schemas.
- `classes/cpp_files/` - C++ Source files implementing the classes logic.
